            "id": "8-1-3",
            "title": "Palora Health's Remuneration Committee Challenge — Full Case Analysis",
            "duration": "10 min",
            "xp": 150,
            "type": "lesson",
            "body": [
              {
                "t": "intro",
                "v": "A new remuneration committee is being asked to set executive pay for the first time as an independent body. This case follows three decisions the committee faces in its first year."
              },
              {
                "t": "scenario",
                "label": "Case Background",
                "title": "Palora Health's First Remcom Year",
                "v": "Palora Health's remuneration committee has three non-executive directors, none with prior executive pay governance experience, supported by the CHRO and an external reward advisor. In their first year, they face three discrete decisions: (1) setting the CEO's pay package for the new year, (2) responding to 22% shareholder opposition to the prior year's bonus payout, and (3) deciding whether to introduce a post-employment shareholding requirement."
              },
              {
                "t": "h",
                "v": "Decision 1 — CEO Package"
              },
              {
                "t": "p",
                "v": "The incoming CEO has asked for a total target compensation of £1.2M, of which £600K is base salary. The external benchmark shows comparable CEOs at a median of £950K total target with £450K base. What should the committee do?"
              },
              {
                "t": "reveal",
                "label": "See the reasoning",
                "v": "The committee should not simply concede to the CEO's ask. A 26% premium to the comparable benchmark at the median requires justification beyond the CEO's own negotiating position — typically, a specific argument about why this role is more complex, has a wider scope, or why the talent market for this specific CEO profile supports the premium. The committee should make an explicit, documented decision: either accept the premium with written rationale, or counter-propose a package closer to benchmark with a specific performance-linked mechanism that allows the CEO to earn above-benchmark total pay if performance warrants it."
              },
              {
                "t": "h",
                "v": "Decision 2 — Responding to 22% Shareholder Opposition"
              },
              {
                "t": "p",
                "v": "22% of shareholders voted against the annual remuneration report. The largest institutional investor sent a letter citing concern about the STI plan's metrics being 'predominantly financial with no ESG element.' How should the committee respond?"
              },
              {
                "t": "reveal",
                "label": "See the reasoning",
                "v": "A 22% opposition is a significant signal by governance standards — it typically triggers a public response from the chair of the remuneration committee acknowledging the vote, confirming engagement with major shareholders, and stating what changes will be considered for next year's policy. Ignoring a 22% vote, or dismissing it without engagement, typically escalates opposition in subsequent years. The specific concern — ESG metrics absent from STI — is substantive and relatively easy to address by introducing a non-financial ESG modifier or weighting in the STI plan for the following year."
              },
              {
                "t": "h",
                "v": "Decision 3 — Post-Employment Shareholding Requirement"
              },
              {
                "t": "p",
                "v": "The external advisor recommends introducing a requirement that executives hold a minimum number of shares for 2 years after leaving the role. The CEO argues this is unusual for a company Palora's size and would put them at a competitive disadvantage when recruiting talent. Should the committee accept this argument?"
              },
              {
                "t": "reveal",
                "label": "See the reasoning",
                "v": "Post-employment shareholding requirements have become standard governance practice in UK listed companies and are increasingly expected by major institutional investors. The CEO's argument that it creates a talent disadvantage is less persuasive than it once was — most comparable executive candidates in this market now expect and understand such requirements as standard. The committee should introduce the requirement while calibrating the shareholding level (typically 100-200% of salary) to be consistent with comparable companies, ensuring it's competitive in design even if it's a new governance feature for Palora specifically."
              },
              {
                "t": "box",
                "label": "What Makes a RemCo Effective",
                "v": "Independence: non-executives who are genuinely independent of management\nInformation quality: access to comparable external benchmark data and internal pay analytics\nStakeholder awareness: understanding of the major shareholders' proxy advisor preferences\nEngagement: proactive shareholder consultation before significant policy changes, not after opposition"
              },
              {
                "t": "takeaways",
                "items": [
                  "A CEO's personal pay ask is a starting point for committee deliberation, not the answer — the committee's role is to test it against benchmark, role complexity, and governance standards.",
                  "A shareholder opposition vote above 20% requires a substantive, public response and genuine policy consideration — not dismissal or a formulaic acknowledgement.",
                  "Post-employment shareholding requirements have become standard governance in listed companies and are increasingly expected by institutional investors regardless of company size."
                ]
              }
            ]
