                "v": "<strong>1. Explain the LTI component's role in the single figure:</strong> The CEO's single figure is significantly influenced by the three-year PSU vesting (£580K), which reflects performance over 2021-2023 rather than current-year pay only. The ratio would be 19.2:1 versus median if the PSU vesting is excluded — illustrating that the ratio reflects multi-year performance rather than current-year salary decisions.\n\n<strong>2. Explain what the CEO's pay is doing:</strong> The LTI component is designed to align the CEO's financial interests with long-term shareholder value — the majority of the CEO's pay is at risk and dependent on exactly the performance that has benefited shareholders over the same period.\n\n<strong>3. Show the employee pay context:</strong> Employee pay at all three percentiles increased by 3.8% in the year, above the 2.9% RPI rate — demonstrating that the board's pay decisions for the broader workforce are also informed by a commitment to real wage growth.\n\n<strong>4. State the policy intent going forward:</strong> The committee is satisfied that the pay ratio reflects the design of the remuneration policy (equity-heavy, performance-contingent) rather than excessive base pay, and expects the ratio to vary year-on-year primarily based on LTI vesting outcomes, which themselves vary with the company's performance."
